WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.990
There are many words that could be used to
describe Wyatt Earp: fearless gunfighter,

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:07.360
uncompromising lawman, deliverer of frontier
justice.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.679
There can also be a few more words like murderer
or con-man or pimp.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:19.180
As you are about to see, Wyatt Earp was a
complex character full of features and foibles.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.130
An icon of the Old West?

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:25.510
Undoubtedly, but calling him a “hero”
might be stretching it a bit.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:30.560
He’s had his good moments, but his life
was also full of questionable actions.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.110
His persona as a paragon of justice in those
wild, ruthless frontier days was carefully

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:43.500
crafted after his death, first by a biography
and then by TV shows, movies, and more books.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.820
Today we will take a look at all the big events
in his life: a 30-second shootout that defined

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:54.269
the Wild West; a quest for revenge taken straight
out of the pages of an adventure novel; a

00:00:54.269 --> 00:00:59.899
boxing match that might have shown us that
everyone could be bought for the right price.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:05.140
Good or bad, these actions helped mold one
of the most captivating and legendary figures

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.500
in American history.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:21.640
Early Life &amp; Family
He was born Wyatt Berry Stapp Earp on March

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:29.030
19, 1848, in Monmouth, Illinois, to Nicholas
Porter Earp and Virginia Ann Cooksey.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:32.900
Wyatt had eight siblings, including one half-brother
from his father’s first marriage.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.130
However, for the sake of brevity, we will
only single out two brothers for now, Virgil

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:42.860
and Morgan Earp, who became gunslinging lawmen
like Wyatt and were right beside him during

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.870
the infamous Gunfight at the O.K. Corral.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:45.870
Image: Young Wyatt and his mom
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wyatt-earp-mother.jpg

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:51.530
The family moved around during Wyatt’s childhood,
first to Pella, Iowa, and then to San Bernardino,

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.530
California.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.060
When the Civil War broke out, his three older
brothers joined the Union Army.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.940
According to legend, Wyatt also wanted to
join the fight, despite being only 13 years

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:00.940
old at the time.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.880
He attempted to run away and enlist several
times, but his father always managed to track

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.740
him down and bring him home.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.610
As a teenager, Wyatt worked various odd jobs,
mainly helping his brothers in driving stagecoaches

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:12.860
and transporting cargo.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.720
He also started gambling around this time,
a lifelong passion of his, and got his first

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.690
experience with refereeing boxing matches,
something that will have a huge impact on

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:24.690
his life much later.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:25.690
Image: Urilla Sutherland
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urilla_Sutherland.jpg

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.290
In his early 20s, Wyatt Earp became a lawman
for the first time.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.640
After a few years of traveling and working
through the West, he settled down and rejoined

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.400
his family who, by then, had relocated again
to Lamar, Missouri.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:42.440
Nicholas Earp was the local constable there,
but in 1869 he resigned his position and Wyatt

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.350
was appointed as his replacement.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:48.800
At that same time, Earp fell in love and married
20-year-old Urilla Sutherland, but their relationship

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:49.800
was short-lived.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.760
She died in 1870, either due to typhoid fever
or childbirth complications.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.410
Downward Spiral
As you might imagine, this loss affected Wyatt

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.410
hard.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.582
First, he started neglecting his duties as
a constable.

00:03:00.582 --> 00:03:04.550
Presumably, at one point, he was either fired
or he quit his job and spent the next few

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.640
years drifting from one city to another, going
from saloon to bar to brothel.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.600
Earp got into a few troubles with the law.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.750
He was accused of horse theft in Arkansas,
was arrested but escaped jail and fled to

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:17.820
Peoria, Illinois.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.220
Here, he reunited with his brother Morgan
and was often reported to be in the company

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:26.810
of a young prostitute named Sally Heckell
who, at times, called herself the wife of

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:27.810
Wyatt Earp.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.320
Going by the charges levied against the Earp
brothers by Peoria lawmen, local authorities

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.500
considered the two to be pimps who ran their
own brothel.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.750
It is possible that the brothers also took
up buffalo hunting, although this is poorly

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:40.750
attested.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:45.250
In fact, the only solid evidence is an entry
in the diary of their young sister, Adelia

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:46.250
Earp.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.920
She wrote that Wyatt and Morgan visited the
family farm on her 11th birthday and brought

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.390
her a lot of pretty clothes because they made
“quite a heap of money” by hunting buffalo.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.820
Of course, it is also plausible that this
was a lie so the brothers could hide their

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.840
less-than-honorable true source of income.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:07.950
Lawman Again in Kansas
Earp’s lawman career picked up again in

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:12.980
1874 when he was hired as an assistant marshal
in Wichita, Kansas.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.720
The most memorable event from this period
was a particularly embarrassing one for a

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.940
man who built his reputation as a gunfighter.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.430
The young Wyatt Earp discharged his revolver
by accident when he dropped it on the floor

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.450
of a gambling house.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:29.260
The bullet passed through his coat and caused
a quick evacuation of the saloon.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.350
Allegedly, Earp confessed to the incident
to his biographer, Stuart Lake, but asked

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.820
that he kept it out of the book.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:40.590
In 1876, Earp left Wichita and joined the
police force in Dodge City, Kansas, a famed

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.690
frontier town during the Wild West.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.550
He stayed there for a few years, except for
a brief spell where he is generally believed

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:49.800
to have traveled to Deadwood, South Dakota,
with his brother, Morgan.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.620
Image: Bat Masterson
Wyatt got into his fair share of scrapes as

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:57.560
a policeman in Dodge City and handled many
of them alongside another noted gunslinger

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:04.870
of the era, Bat Masterson, who we might see
on this channel some day.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:11.180
In 1877, Wyatt Earp left in pursuit of outlaw
Dave Rudabaugh after the former robbed a railroad

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.320
construction camp.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.460
He never caught up to the criminal, but the
chase was noteworthy for a different reason

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:20.100
- Earp met and befriended fellow gambler and
gunslinger Doc Holliday.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.030
Image: Doc Holliday
On another occasion, Wyatt Earp, Bat Masterson,

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:27.220
and several other people got into a gunfight
with a drunken posse that was shooting up

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.220
the town.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.730
One of the gunmen named George Hoy or Hoyt
was injured in the shootout and later died

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:37.870
with many historians giving Earp the credit
and touting George Hoy as the first man that

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.090
Wyatt Earp killed.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.050
However, there was never any surefire evidence
showing who actually hit him.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.960
Another close call happened around September,
1878, when Ed Morrison and a rowdy posse of

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:51.680
25 other men were causing a ruckus at the
Long Branch Saloon.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.480
Earp went to confront them and was aiming
his gun at Morrison when one of his many compatriots

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.060
reached for his own weapon.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:02.150
Fortunately, he never got a shot off because
he was hit in the shoulder by none other than

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:07.340
Doc Holliday who had since relocated to Dodge
City and was gambling at the Long Branch Saloon

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.330
when he stepped in to save his friend.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.850
That is a nice story, but it probably never
happened.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:17.520
Although Earp did say that Holliday once saved
his life in Dodge City, there are no police

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.760
records or newspaper stories of this event
even though the city’s history is, otherwise,

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.000
well-documented.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.710
This seems like a good place to mention that
many of these lesser-known anecdotes from

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:33.520
Wyatt Earp’s life come to us from a single
source - the biography of the aforementioned

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.120
Stuart Lake titled “Wyatt Earp: Frontier
Marshal”.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.400
Published in 1931, the book was absolutely
instrumental in creating the Wild West persona

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.700
of Wyatt Earp that we know today.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:49.960
However, calling it a “biography” is rather
deceitful since it has about as much fiction

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.010
as it does fact.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.700
Image: Colt Buntline Special
A perfect example of this is the Colt Buntline

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:55.720
Special.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:59.850
This is one of the most famous guns of the
Wild West because it was the gun of Wyatt

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.440
Earp and it had a distinctive 12-inch barrel.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:08.040
However, the only problem is that the gun
did not exist; not in Wyatt’s time, at least.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.330
Lake described the weapon in detail and told
a good story of how it was custom-ordered

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:17.850
by dime novelist Ned Buntline who gave away
five of these to famed Old West lawmen.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:22.360
It was popular enough to create a long-lasting
myth that still raises doubts today.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.600
Colt eventually did start producing this gun
during the 1950s, because of its popularity

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:32.919
and connection to Wyatt Earp, and today it
is a prized artifact for gun collectors.

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:40.270
Arrival in Tombstone
We got a bit off-track, but it is important

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:45.260
to remember how easy it was to fabricate details
about larger-than-life figures whose exploits

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.190
were told mainly through newspaper stories
and pulp novels.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.430
Now let’s get back to the good stuff as
Wyatt Earp and his brothers relocated to the

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.050
boomtown of Tombstone, Arizona.

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:58.020
Image: Mattie Blaylock
He did so in December 1879, accompanied by

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:02.070
Mattie Blaylock, a former prostitute who became
his common-law wife.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:06.630
Tombstone had only been founded earlier that
year once prospectors discovered silver in

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:07.630
the area.

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:11.170
In just several months, the community grew
from 100 or so people living in shacks and

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:14.300
tents to a proper town with over 1,000 residents.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:19.010
Older brother Virgil Earp was the town marshal
while Wyatt and Morgan worked as private security

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:23.449
by riding shotgun, quite literally, on Wells
Fargo stagecoaches.

00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:27.810
Siblings James and Warren Earp also moved
to Tombstone but, for the most part, stayed

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.070
out of the gunslinging shenanigans.

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:34.630
Rounding up their little posse was Doc Holliday
who came a little later.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:39.310
It didn’t take long for the Earps to confront
the Cowboys, a group of outlaws who would

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.310
quickly grow to despise them.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:45.820
Rather than being a traditional gang, the
Cowboys were more of a primitive crime syndicate

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:47.570
active in the Arizona Territory.

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:52.170
They numbered hundreds of members that were
only loosely-affiliated with one another.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:55.720
For now, we will only mention a few of them
who played prominent roles in Earp’s story.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.080
They were the Clanton brothers, Ike, Billy,
and Phineas; Frank Stillwell, Pete Spence,

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:05.520
Curly Bill Brocius, Frank and Tom McLaury,
Billy Claiborne, and Johnny Ringo.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.480
Tensions kept rising as the two groups had
run-ins with each other.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.920
Things took a serious turn in September, 1881,
when two of the Cowboys, Stillwell and Spence,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:17.950
robbed a stagecoach on the Sandy Bob Line
to Bisbee, Arizona.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:22.760
They were arrested by a posse which included
two Earps, but were released.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:27.620
Virgil Earp arrested them again a few weeks
later, this time on a federal charge of interfering

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:28.750
with a mail shipment.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:34.600
They were again let go for insufficient evidence,
but this act angered the local Cowboys, particularly

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:35.600
the McLaurys.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:40.170
They claimed they would kill the Earps if
they tried to arrest them again.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:48.550
Gunfight at the O.K. Corral
After weeks of threats, the escalating situation

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.620
came to a head on October 26, 1881.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:56.040
That morning, an incident between Wyatt Earp
and Tom McLaury led to the former pistol whipping

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.160
the latter for allegedly carrying a concealed
weapon.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:02.820
Throughout the rest of the day, McLaury gathered
more of his partners and the Cowboys were

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.390
seen loitering angrily around town.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:09.210
According to a recently-passed ordinance,
people had to surrender any deadly weapons

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.990
upon entering Tombstone.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.850
Marshal Virgil Earp intended to disarm the
Cowboys and asked Wyatt, Morgan, and Doc Holliday

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:16.850
to assist him.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:21.510
Five of them had gathered on Fremont Street,
in a vacant lot near the O.K. Corral.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:26.250
They were Frank and Tom McLaury, Billy and
Ike Clanton, and Billy Claiborne.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:29.430
At around 3 p.m., the Earps confronted the
Cowboys.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:34.420
According to his later testimony, Virgil demanded
they surrender their weapons, but two of them

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:35.800
cocked their revolvers.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.560
Then...the shooting started.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:41.940
It is impossible to say with any certainty
what the exact sequence of events was.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:46.630
The gunfight at the O.K. Corral lasted no
more than 30 seconds, time during which around

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:50.120
30 shots were fired, filling the lot with
black gunpowder smoke.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.620
At the end, Billy Clanton, Frank and Tom McLaury
were dead.

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:58.770
Ike Clanton and Billy Claiborne were, likely,
unarmed and managed to run away right before

00:10:58.770 --> 00:10:59.990
the shooting started.

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:04.430
Virgil and Morgan Earp and Doc Holliday had
been shot, but their injuries were not serious.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:09.720
Wyatt Earp alone stood unscathed.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:16.420
Aftermath
Just 30 seconds created the most famous gunfight

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:21.180
in the history of the Wild West, although
newspapers had a heavy influence on how it

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:22.180
was remembered.

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:27.279
For starters, despite its name, it never actually
took place at the O.K. Corral, but rather

00:11:27.279 --> 00:11:28.279
next to it.

00:11:28.279 --> 00:11:32.270
Also, the whole thing was more Virgil’s
affair rather than Wyatt’s but, for some

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:35.300
reason, the media preferred to focus on the
younger brother.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:40.620
Maybe it was because he was the one left standing
or maybe this extra attention on Wyatt Earp

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:45.240
was only applied retroactively as he continued
to have more headline-making adventures while

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.260
Virgil, for the most part, led a rather unassuming
life from then on.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:53.480
Either way, one part that usually gets left
out of the story is the legal aftermath that

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.910
came following the shootout.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:59.790
A few days later, Ike Clanton filed murder
charges against the Earps and Doc Holliday.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:03.589
As Morgan and Virgil were still recuperating
from their wounds, only Wyatt and Holliday

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:07.810
were arrested and brought to court before
Judge Wells Spicer.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:12.950
There were plenty of testimonies but, unsurprisingly,
they were often contradictory and were dependent

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:15.080
on who the witness sympathized with.

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:19.741
A lot of the focus was on who shot first,
but most sources agreed that the first two

00:12:19.741 --> 00:12:25.779
shots which, ostensibly, belonged to Billy
Clanton and Wyatt Earp, happened almost simultaneously.

00:12:25.779 --> 00:12:29.910
One historian who carefully compared all the
neutral testimonies concluded that, by and

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.280
large, they supported the account put forward
by the Earps that they tried to disarm the

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:39.440
Cowboys and the gunfight erupted at the provocation
of Clanton and Frank McLaury.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:44.709
Judge Spicer agreed and cleared the Earps
and Holliday of any wrongdoing.

00:12:44.709 --> 00:12:51.339
Cowboys’ Revenge
The Earps won the fight, but they knew that

00:12:51.339 --> 00:12:54.280
there were other Cowboys out there who would
be looking for revenge.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:58.029
As a precaution, the brothers moved their
families to the Cosmopolitan Hotel so that

00:12:58.029 --> 00:12:59.029
they would be safer together.

00:12:59.029 --> 00:13:02.500
Image: Virgil Earp
At around midnight on December 28, 1881, Virgil

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:07.550
Earp was returning home to the hotel when
he was ambushed by several men carrying shotguns.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:12.510
They unloaded three or four shots of buckshot
into him, hitting Virgil in the left arm,

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:13.620
hip, and groin.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.240
He stumbled into the hotel and, against all
odds, the doctor managed to save his life,

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.860
although Virgil permanently lost the use of
his left arm.

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:25.550
His shooters were never identified, although
strong suspects included the remaining Clanton

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:27.399
brothers, Pete Spence, and Johnny Ringo.

00:13:27.399 --> 00:13:29.990
Image: Morgan Earp
The reprisals were not over.

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.020
Almost three months later, Morgan Earp was
ambushed in a similar way.

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:38.200
On March 18, 1882, he was inside a billiards
parlor playing pool alongside Wyatt and a

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.610
few of their cohorts.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:43.500
Shots came in through the window from one
or more unidentified assailants.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:47.540
One of the bullets hit Morgan in the spine
and pierced his left kidney.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:49.580
Another one hit the wall inches from Wyatt’s
head.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:53.980
A doctor was brought in but it was clear that
the injuries were fatal.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:57.740
According to Stuart Lake, Wyatt and Morgan
often talked about people having “visions

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:02.350
of heaven” on their deathbed and Morgan’s
last words were whispered in his brother’s

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:06.610
ear, saying “I can’t see a damn thing”.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:13.510
Earp’s Vendetta Ride
In between the two attacks, Wyatt Earp had

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:18.000
been made deputy U.S. marshal to replace his
brother, Virgil, who was no longer able to

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.279
fulfil his duties.

00:14:19.279 --> 00:14:23.519
After the first ambush, Earp still held out
hope that the system would deliver justice

00:14:23.519 --> 00:14:24.670
to the men responsible.

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:29.269
However, there was a dearth of evidence, not
to mention the fact that any suspect had ten

00:14:29.269 --> 00:14:31.810
other Cowboys ready to provide alibis for
them.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:35.880
After Morgan was killed, Wyatt Earp realized
that he will not have justice, but he could

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.600
still have vengeance.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:42.040
He embarked on a crusade where he gathered
a posse of men he trusted and who knew how

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:44.730
to handle a gun and set off to take out the
Cowboys.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:49.050
It became known in western lore as Earp’s
vendetta ride.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:52.250
It did not take long for Earp’s posse to
claim their first victim.

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:56.500
The day after Morgan’s death, his body was
put on a train and sent to Colton, California,

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.570
where his widow and parents were.

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:02.700
Also on the train were Virgil and his wife
who were leaving Tombstone as it simply became

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:03.769
too dangerous for them.

00:15:03.769 --> 00:15:05.880
Image: Frank Stillwell
Wyatt received information that some of the

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:09.530
Cowboys were in Tucson where the train would
make a long stop.

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:13.740
He believed they were waiting to finish off
Virgil so he and his men accompanied them.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:18.560
Indeed, the Cowboys were there, but made no
attempt on Virgil’s life as he was surrounded

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:21.460
at all times by five men armed to the teeth.

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:25.680
When his train departed, people heard multiple
gunshots coming from the train yard and saw

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:27.570
men running away from the scene.

00:15:27.570 --> 00:15:33.000
Left behind they discovered the riddled body
of Frank Stillwell, a Cowboy and chief suspect

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:34.570
in Morgan’s murder.

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:38.750
According to Wyatt Earp’s version of events
given decades after the fact, he saw Stillwell

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:42.680
and Ike Clanton waiting in ambush on a flatcar
in the train yard.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:47.150
He and his men went after them and the Cowboys
ran away, but Stillwell stumbled and got left

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:48.150
behind.

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:52.480
Earp caught up to him and discharged both
shotgun barrels into Stillwell when he tried

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:53.920
to grab the weapon.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:54.920
The other gunmen shot Stillwell’s corpse
some more for good measure.

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:55.920
Image: Earp and Holliday statues where Stillwell
was killed

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:56.920
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tucson-Amtrak_Station-1907-1922-2.JPG
Shockingly, even back in the Wild West, people

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:58.870
weren’t ok with Earp’s extra-judicial
killing.

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:03.140
Five men were indicted for Stillwell’s murder
and arrest warrants were issued for Wyatt

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:08.950
and Warren Earp, Doc Holliday, Sherman McMaster
and Jack “Turkey Creek” Johnson.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:13.220
These five can be considered the original
members of the vendetta ride alongside “Texas

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:17.700
Jack” Vermillion who didn’t travel to
Tucson and stayed behind with the horses.

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:20.390
Dan Tipton and Charlie Smith joined them later.

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:24.440
Although they were now wanted men, the vendetta
ride continued into the Dragoon Mountains.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:29.760
They caught up and killed a Cowboy named Florentino
Cruz who, according to Wyatt, served as lookout

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.540
the night of Morgan’s murder.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:35.790
Unbeknownst to Earp, two other men suspected
of being involved, Pete Spence and an Indian

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:39.320
known as Hank, had already been arrested on
other charges.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.959
Spence even turned himself in because he realized
it was safer in jail.

00:16:42.959 --> 00:16:45.770
Image: Curly Bill Brocius
The vendetta ride ended with a bang in Iron

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:47.570
Springs in the Whetstone Mountains.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:52.459
On March 24, 1882, they caught up to a Cowboys
posse that made camp there.

00:16:52.459 --> 00:16:57.529
A shootout ensued and Earp recognized Curly
Bill Brocius as being among the gunmen.

00:16:57.529 --> 00:17:01.750
According to Wyatt, he almost cut Brocius
in two with a double-barrel shotgun blast

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:02.779
to the stomach.

00:17:02.779 --> 00:17:06.819
Another Cowboy named Johnny Barnes was also
killed in the gunfight although, miraculously,

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:08.459
none of Earp’s men were even injured.

00:17:08.459 --> 00:17:12.309
There were still a few Cowboys who might have
been involved in the attacks on the Earps,

00:17:12.309 --> 00:17:15.709
but they met their own violent, predictable
ends.

00:17:15.709 --> 00:17:20.659
That same year, Billy Claiborne was killed
in Tombstone by “Buckskin” Frank Leslie.

00:17:20.659 --> 00:17:24.829
Johnny Ringo was found dead of an apparent
suicide although, for obvious reasons, people

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:26.439
also suspected murder.

00:17:26.439 --> 00:17:30.830
Phineas and Ike Clanton moved away and, a
few years later, were charged with cattle

00:17:30.830 --> 00:17:31.830
rustling.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:34.649
Phineas was taken in and Ike was killed resisting
arrest.

00:17:34.649 --> 00:17:38.749
There were surprisingly few consequences for
Earp for his vendetta ride.

00:17:38.749 --> 00:17:43.539
Some of the men were arrested, but released
on bail and they left the Arizona territory.

00:17:43.539 --> 00:17:45.910
Further attempts to extradite them were unsuccessful.

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:51.019
Wyatt Earp moved to San Francisco with his
new partner, Josephine Marcus, and left the

00:17:51.019 --> 00:17:52.019
Wild West behind.

00:17:52.019 --> 00:17:53.019
Image: Dodge City Peace Commission
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DodgeCityPeaceCommission.jpg

00:17:53.019 --> 00:17:56.739
In 1883, Earp made a brief return to his old
ways that is worthy of inclusion when his

00:17:56.739 --> 00:18:01.619
friend and fellow gunfighter Luke Short asked
for his help in a conflict known as the Dodge

00:18:01.619 --> 00:18:02.629
City War.

00:18:02.629 --> 00:18:07.100
It ended up being bloodless and mostly uneventful,
but it is memorable for giving us one of the

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:11.970
most iconic photographs of the Wild West with
Short and his assembled posse.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.710
Known as the Dodge City Peace Commission,
it included some of the west’s most famed

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:20.559
gunslingers such as Short himself, Wyatt Earp,
of course, Bat Masterson, and Charlie Bassett.

00:18:20.559 --> 00:18:25.769
As you can probably tell, Wyatt Earp is the
one with the hat and moustache.

00:18:25.769 --> 00:18:29.009
Fitzsimmons vs. Sharkey
In his post-Tombstone life, Earp tried his

00:18:29.009 --> 00:18:33.470
hand in many ventures such as mining, oil,
horse racing, and saloon ownership.

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:37.679
He even served as a Hollywood consultant when
he lived in Los Angeles, but none of these

00:18:37.679 --> 00:18:40.669
had memorable moments that need mentioning.

00:18:40.669 --> 00:18:45.539
One job that did get the people talking big-time,
though, was his stint as a boxing referee.

00:18:45.539 --> 00:18:51.090
In 1896, the boxing world was left without
a clear undisputed champion after the previous

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.940
titleholder, James J. Corbett, retired.

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:58.779
Eventually, one match scheduled for December
2 at the Mechanics Pavilion in San Francisco

00:18:58.779 --> 00:18:59.830
would settle the matter.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:04.789
The fighters were Tom Sharkey and Robert Fitzsimmons,
but organizers were having trouble finding

00:19:04.789 --> 00:19:07.070
a referee that both sides accepted.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:11.739
In the end, they offered the gig to Wyatt
Earp who had the experience and reputation.

00:19:11.739 --> 00:19:15.889
The fight ended with a controversial finish
when Fitzsimmons hit Sharkey with a punch

00:19:15.889 --> 00:19:17.279
in the 8th round.

00:19:17.279 --> 00:19:19.999
Sharkey fell to the floor, clutching his groin
in agony.

00:19:19.999 --> 00:19:24.610
Earp disqualified Fitzsimmons for a low blow
and gave the victory and the $10,000 purse

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:25.679
that came with it to Sharkey.

00:19:25.679 --> 00:19:26.710
Image: “Bad Man” Referee caricature
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad_man_wyatt_earp_in_cartoon.png

00:19:26.710 --> 00:19:31.109
The problem was that almost everyone else
saw the punch as a legitimate hit to the solar

00:19:31.109 --> 00:19:32.109
plexus.

00:19:32.109 --> 00:19:35.639
There was immediate talk of the match being
fixed with Earp in on the scam.

00:19:35.639 --> 00:19:39.289
An injunction was placed on the money and
the fight moved to the courtroom.

00:19:39.289 --> 00:19:43.629
Even though Earp was acquitted of any fixing
charges, he remained guilty in the eyes of

00:19:43.629 --> 00:19:44.629
the public.

00:19:44.629 --> 00:19:48.740
In his lifetime, this event severely denigrated
the reputation of Wyatt Earp and accusations

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:52.879
followed him until he died in 1929 of liver
disease at age 80.

00:19:52.879 --> 00:19:57.470
It wasn’t until Lake published his biography
that Wyatt Earp regained a positive image,

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:09.389
although it was a fanciful and glowing account
that was only tangentially connected

00:20:09.389 --> 00:20:26.419
to reality.

